<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vortiporius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. mid-6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Gwrthefyr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vortiporius was a 6th-century king of Dyfed in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a> called tyrant of the Demetae and condemned in On the Ruin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A memorial stone from Castell Dwyran in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and ogham to Voteporigis the Protector, is often taken to be his. Welsh genealogies name him Gwrthefyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vortiporius.jpg" alt="Vortiporius" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LinguisticDemographer at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LDDyfedCantrefi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
